Reply posted separately to give those whose memories
don't go back to where the web began some sense of
history. No one who has used "the net" since it
first got networked would have any problems
understanding that "the web" is all the things
browsers connect together, just like all the twigs
to which the spiderweb from which that term was
coined connects are part of that web, even though
not made of spider silk.

> www.flowers.com

And yes, in a very real sense, the goods and
services moving as part of internet commerce, the
advertising that sustains that commerce, are very
much "part of the web"; in fact, they are the money
machines that keep it running, as integrated as it
is possible for them to be.

Notice here, for example, how entirely "internet",
"web", "things done using the web" have become
conflated in common usage:

http://www.google.com/search?hl=en&q=define%3Aecommerce

That usage trend will only accellerate.

Notice also that the wikipedia definition of "the
web" as an "information space" is much more
servicable than most definitions found, and is very
much in agreement with "any protocol a URI can
invoke", "any resource that can be accessed by a
URI":

  The World Wide Web ("WWW", "W3", or simply "Web")
  is an information space in which the items of
  interest, referred to as resources, are identified
  by global identifiers called Uniform Resource
  Identifiers (URI). The term is often mistakenly
  used as a synonym for the Internet, but the Web is
  actually a service that operates over the
  Internet.

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_web

